# AutoX session service access

Session-scoped services (PluginManager, ExecutionContextManager, DebugFlowManager, StepsExecutor, ReportDataManager, ExecutionPool, EventManager, LogMonitorManager, AutomationEventManager, ApplicationConfiguration, PrefixExpressionFactory customs, etc.) must be obtained from `AutomationContext`, not via static helpers on the service class.

`PrefixExpressionFactory`: Java-annotated parsers are process-level (shared). XML/custom prefixes and UI locators are session-scoped on the context factory and cleared via `clearCustomExpressions()` on context reset. Prefer `AutomationContext.getPrefixExpressionFactory()` (or `PrefixExpressionFactory.getExpressionFactory()` which delegates to context).

Dynamic session services (e.g. mock servers) implement `IDynAutomationService` and are registered on the context by unique name (`registerDynAutomationService` / `getDynAutomationService` / `unregisterDynAutomationService`). They are closed on context reset.

```java
// BAD
DebugFlowManager.get().sendClientMessage(msg);
PluginManager.getInstance().getPlugins();

// GOOD
AutomationContext.getInstance().getDebugFlowManager()...
AutomationContext.getInstance().getPluginManager()...
```

## Capture fixed instances in the constructor

When a class's lifetime is bound to one session (e.g. `LiveDebugPoint`, debug server handlers) and `AutomationContext` / managers will not change for that instance, resolve them once in the constructor and store as `final` fields:

```java
private final AutomationContext automationContext;
private final DebugFlowManager debugFlowManager;

public LiveDebugPoint(...)
{
	this.automationContext = AutomationContext.getInstance();
	this.debugFlowManager = (DebugFlowManager) automationContext.getDebugFlowManager();
	// ...
}
```

Do not re-call `AutomationContext.getInstance()` repeatedly inside such classes unless the lookup must reflect a possible context change.
